A write that moves an effect's key list settles it from inside a nested block.

`injectSettles` asks allBlocks for every block a write can sit in and puts the
settle call after each write it finds. A *view* body arrives branch by branch --
the collector descends its control flow and adds each one -- but an imperative
block arrives as a single pointer, so the `if` inside a handler or inside a tick
body was never visited and the write in it settled nothing.

Both shapes are here, one brace deeper than `timer_enabled_toggle.txtar`, which
writes the gate at the top of its handler and so passed throughout:

  - `@click { if seconds > 0 { running = !running } }` -- the countdown never
    starts, though the label updates, because the reactive updater injection
    does descend and this one did not.
  - `@tick { seconds -= 1; if seconds <= 0 { running = false } }` -- the
    countdown never stops itself, which is the same bug in the effect's own
    mount body.

This is the tutorial "timers" regression a second time. It became reachable for
html when the timer stopped being a primitive: the emitter's own re-sync used
codegen.MutatedFields, which walks a statement recursively.

deny out/none/html/index.html `state.running = !state.running;\n *\}` -- the write is followed by the settle, not by the end of the branch
deny out/none/html/index.html `state.running = false;\n *\}` -- likewise in the tick body

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var seconds = 3
    var running = false

    timer(interval=1000ms, enabled=running, @tick {
        seconds -= 1
        if seconds <= 0 {
            running = false
        }
    })
    vbox {
        text(value="{seconds} s")
        button(text="start", @click {
            if seconds > 0 {
                running = !running
            }
        })
    }
}

window {
    main
}
